%I A397742 #23 Aug 08 2026 21:16:40
%S A397742 8,18,27,40,56,75,88,90,104,125,126,136,147,152,184,198,232,234,245,
%T A397742 248,280,296,297,306,328,342,343,344,351,376,414,424,440,459,472,488,
%U A397742 513,520,522,525,536,558,568,584,605,616,621,630,632,664,666,680,712,728
%N A397742 Numbers k such that A008479(k) = 3.
%C A397742 Classify numbers k by their squarefree kernel, A007947(k) = rad(k) = j. There are infinitely many k for each squarefree number, j, except 1. These classes are listed in the columns of square tables A284311 and A285321. If, instead, we look at the rows of either of these tables, we see that row i contains the numbers k with A008479(k) = i, so that we are now classifying numbers by having i-1 lesser numbers that share the same squarefree kernel. We wish to specify the forms of numbers in each class for low values of i. For i = 1 we simply get the squarefree numbers, A005117. Here i = 3.
%C A397742 Numbers k such that there exist exactly 2 other numbers m < k that share the squarefree kernel A007947(k) = rad(k).
%C A397742 Without prime cubes, this sequence is a proper subset of A332785.
%C A397742 We may partition this sequence into 2 blocks as follows:
%C A397742   A. Numbers k of the form rad(k)*lpf(k)^2.
%C A397742   B. Numbers k of the form rad(k)*A119288(k).
%C A397742 For each value of rad(k) there is clearly exactly one number in the sequence. If rad(k) has a prime factor q satisfying lpf(k) < q < lpf(k)^2 the relevant k is in block B, otherwise it is in block A.
%C A397742 A030078 is a subset of block A.
%C A397742 Smallest term k with i > 1 distinct prime factors is 3*A002110(i), 3 times the product of i smallest prime factors. These terms are in A055932 and belong to block B.
%H A397742 Michael De Vlieger, <a href="/A397742/b397742.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A397742 {a(n)} = {A065642(A397710(j)) : j >= 1}.
%e A397742 18 = 2 * 3^2 is greater than 6 = 2 * 3 and 12 = 2^2 * 3, with 6 and 12 being the only 2 smaller numbers that share the squarefree kernel of 18 (in other words, they are divisible by exactly the same prime numbers). So 18 is in the sequence.
%e A397742 24 = 2^3 * 3 is greater than 6 = 2 * 3, 12 = 2^2 * 3 and 18 = 2 * 3^2, making 3 smaller numbers that share the squarefree kernel of 24. So 24 is not in the sequence.
%e A397742 40 = 2^3 * 5 is greater than 10 = 2 * 5, 20 = 2^2 * 5 (but not 50 = 2 * 5^2), and 10 and 20 are the only 2 smaller numbers that share the squarefree kernel of 40. So 40 is in the sequence.
%e A397742 Table of n, a(n) for select n:
%e A397742        n   block   a(n)
%e A397742   --------------------------------------------------------
%e A397742        1   A         8 = 2^3
%e A397742        2   B        18 = 2   *  3^2
%e A397742        3   A        27 = 3^3
%e A397742        4   A        40 = 2^3 *  5
%e A397742        5   A        56 = 2^3 *  7
%e A397742        6   B        75 = 3   *  5^2
%e A397742        7   A        88 = 2^3 * 11
%e A397742        8   B        90 = 2   *  3^2 * 5
%e A397742       23   A       297 = 3^3 * 11
%e A397742       45   B       605 = 5   * 11^2
%e A397742       48   B       630 = 2   *  3^2 * 5 * 7
%e A397742      512   B      6930 = 2   *  3^2 * 5 * 7 * 11
%e A397742     6604   B     90090 = 2   *  3^2 * 5 * 7 * 11 * 13
%e A397742   112144   B   1531530 = 2   *  3^2 * 5 * 7 * 11 * 13 * 17
%t A397742 rad[x_] := Times @@ FactorInteger[x][[All, 1]]; Select[Range[750], Function[{k, r}, Count[Range[k], _?(rad[#] == r &)] == 3] @@ {#, rad[#]} &]
%Y A397742 Cf. A008479, A020639, A065642.
%Y A397742 Row 3 of A284311 (or A285321), sorted.
%Y A397742 Sequences for other rows: A005117 (1), A397710 (2).
%Y A397742 See the comments for the relationships with A002110, A007947, A030078, A055932, A119288, A332785.
